its safe level to buy town centre properties","lead":"Harlow Council's borrowing has risen to \u00a3347.3m, exceeding what it deems a prudent borrowing level\u2014the liability benchmark\u2014of \u00a3333.2m by \u00a314.1m. The increase stems largely from property acquisitions including Market House, Rothwell House, 4 Wych Elm and Terminus House, adding \u00a334m to the council's debt. Despite the extra borrowing, the council achieved a credit rating improvement from A+ to AA\u2014now above its peer average\u2014and negotiated an interest rate of 3.683%, below the 4.0% budgeted. However, interest costs exceeded budget by \u00a31.65m because actual borrowing proved substantially higher than forecast. The council itself acknowledges strategies involving borrowing significantly above the prudent level \"carry higher risk\".","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"DESIGNATION OF SECTION 151 OFFICER","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31432/31432.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Harlow faces housing regulator enforcement after safety failures, with audit work backlog growing.","lead":"Harlow Council received a formal non-compliance finding from the Regulator of Social Housing in September 2024 for serious failures in safety standards, exposing the authority to potential prosecutions, fines and compensation claims. The council is running remediation programmes to address fire risk assessments, water safety, gas safety and electrical safety, but faces resource constraints. The Audit & Standards Committee heard this week that 12 audit recommendations have passed their implementation due dates, including a high-priority finding on procurement training for housing staff arising from a whistleblower investigation. The backlogs have grown since February; finance team vacancies are also preventing completion of the statutory audit of the council's financial statements, which now faces an earlier deadline of 31 January 2027.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Audit - 14 July 2026","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31399/31399.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31532/31532.pdf","headline":"Council has advanced \u00a37.1m to subsidiaries: \u00a31.5m to HTS for vehicles at 8.23%, and \u00a35.6m to Market Square LLP","detail":"HTS (Property and Environment) Ltd, a wholly owned subsidiary, borrowed \u00a31.8m in 2024/25 for vehicle purchases. By 31 March 2026, \u00a30.3m had been repaid, leaving \u00a31.5m outstanding at 8.23% interest, due over 5 years. Market Square LLP received \u00a35.6m in 2024/25 with no repayments recorded during 2025/26 and no new lending made. These are concentrated counterparty exposures to subsidiaries rather than external lenders.","category":"governance","matters":"Subsidiary lending concentrates credit risk; council's ability to service its own debt depends on subsidiaries' performance and capacity to repay","figures":"\u00a31.5m outstanding to HTS at 8.23%; \u00a35.6m outstanding to Market Square LLP","quote":"The Authority loaned HTS \u00a31.8m in 2024/25 for the purchase of new vehicles. The first repayment was made in 2025/26 and the balance outstanding at 31st March 2026 for principal and interest was \u00a31.5m. In 2024/25, the Authority loaned \u00a35.6m to its subsidiary Market Square LLP. No further non-treasury investments were made during 2025/26.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31532/31532.pdf","headline":"CCLA property fund investment impaired to \u00a31.8m from \u00a32m principal (10% loss), though statutory override masks accounting impact","detail":"The council's \u00a32m investment in CCLA Property Fund from 2017/18 has declined to \u00a31.8m (\u00a30.2m or 10% loss). Royal London Cash Plus fund remains at \u00a32m. The statutory override on accounting for pooled fund gains and losses has been extended to April 2029, protecting the council's accounts from reflecting these unrealized losses. The council maintains a Treasury Management Reserve to cover any future impact after the override expires.","category":"other","matters":"Impairment signals stress in commercial property markets; statutory override provides temporary relief but future exposure remains if asset values don't recover","figures":"CCLA fund: \u00a32m principal, \u00a31.8m value (10% loss); Royal London: \u00a32m principal and value","quote":"The Authority initially invested \u00a32m principal into CCLA Property fund and \u00a32m into Royal London Cash Plus fund in 2017/18... However, the value as at 31st March 2026 of these investments were \u00a31.8m for CCLA Property fund and \u00a32m for Royal London Cash Plus fund.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31532/31532.pdf","headline":"Interest costs exceeded budget by \u00a31.65m (11.1m actual vs 9.5m budgeted), despite actual rates lower than expected","detail":"Interest costs came in at \u00a311.141m against a budget of \u00a39.491m. The variance of \u00a31.65m adverse arose despite the actual average borrowing rate (3.683%) being 32 basis points better than budgeted (4.000%). This indicates the council borrowed significantly more than was forecast in the budget. The actual average principal borrowed was \u00a3300.161m against an expected \u00a3308.5m \u2013 suggesting the mix of borrowing changed from expectations, or cash flows differed.","category":"overspend","matters":"Interest cost overruns reduce resources available for council services; indicates forecasting challenges that may persist in future years","figures":"Actual interest \u00a311.141m vs budget \u00a39.491m (variance \u00a31.650m); actual rate 3.683% vs budget 4.000%","quote":"Actual interest \u00a3m 11.141 ... Budgeted interest \u00a3m 9.491 ... Variance \u00a3m 1.650","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31532/31532.pdf","headline":"Borrowing of \u00a3347.3m exceeds the prudent liability benchmark of \u00a3333.2m by \u00a314.1m","detail":"The liability benchmark represents the prudent level of borrowing needed to fund capital plans while maintaining minimum cash reserves. Harlow's actual borrowing now exceeds this benchmark. The council itself notes that strategies involving borrowing significantly above the benchmark carry higher risk, though it observes that borrowing may be justified by the capital programme. Forecasts show borrowing declining back toward benchmark levels as the capital programme completes.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Borrowing above prudent benchmarks constrains financial flexibility and signals higher refinancing and interest rate risk","figures":"Actual borrowing \u00a3347.3m vs liability benchmark \u00a3333.2m (excess of \u00a314.1m); forecast existing borrowing 31.3.27: \u00a3245.5m","quote":"Whilst borrowing may be above the liability benchmark, strategies involving borrowing which is significantly above the liability benchmark carry higher risk.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31532/31532.pdf","headline":"Harlow's credit rating improved from A+ to AA-, now above peer group and all-LA average","detail":"Independent credit assessment by Arlingclose improved: credit score moved from 4.68 to 4.48 (lower is better); bail-in exposure reduced from 72% to 64%. The council's AA- rating now exceeds both similar local authorities (A+) and the cross-LA average (A+). This reflects improved credit quality of the treasury investment portfolio.","category":"governance","matters":"Credit rating improvements indicate stronger financial position and lower default risk; independent validation of financial strength is material to lender assessment","figures":"Credit rating A+ to AA-; credit score 4.68 to 4.48; bail-in exposure 72% to 64%","quote":"The independently assessed credit quality of the council's treasury investments also improved, with Harlow's credit rating increasing from A+ to AA-, placing the council above both similar local authorities and the average across all local authorities.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Harlow","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Council has advanced \u00a37.1m to subsidiaries: \u00a31.5m to HTS for vehicles at 8.23%, and \u00a35.6m to Market Square LLP","lead":"Subsidiary lending concentrates credit risk; council's ability to service its own debt depends on subsidiaries' performance and capacity to repay","also":["25 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-29","n_findings":26,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix A - Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","top_paper_url":"https://harlow.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s31532/31532.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":112,"news_drafts_total":2}}